Ok, in response to the multiple "That candle/book/bell thing is from
<insert PC game>, right?" questions, I submit the following, reprinted
without permission, from _The Norton Anthology of English Literature,
Fifth Edition_:

From "Dr. Faustus" by Christopher Marlowe, Act 3, Scene 2, Lines 95-98:

Mephistophilis:  "Now, Faustus, what will you do now?  For I can tell
you/ you'll be cursed with bell, book and candle."

Faustus:  "Bell, book, candle; candle, book, and bell/  Forward and
backward, to curse Faustus to Hell!"

There is a footnote regarding this passage:  "The traditional
paraphanalia for cursing and excommunication."

So even though there have been MANY variations on this is computer
games in the past, this is the original.  Marlowe's completion date of
1616 predates all of these, even Zork I.  :)

